HEARD l00 a tidal wave of syrup flooded Boston.
HEARD l01 19, 35 miles an hour.
HEARD l02 It started with one giant tank, 50 feet tall, full of molasses.
HEARD l03 The man in charge of building it was not an engineer. He was the company's accountant.
HEARD l04 The accountant could not even read the blueprints.
HEARD l05 He never tested it properly so it leaked from day one.
HEARD l06 His solution, he painted the tank brown.
HEARD l07 So the leaks would match.
HEARD l08 The neighbors just showed up with buckets and took the free molasses home.
HEARD l09 then came one warm day in January.
HEARD l10 The tank started groaning.
HEARD l11 rivets popped out like machine gun fire.
HEARD l12 and two million gallons came out at once.
HEARD l13 The wave knocked a streetcar off its tracks and threw a truck into the harbor.
HEARD l14 One boy rode the top of it, like a surfer.
HEARD l15 21 people died, rescuers rushed in, and the cold turned the syrup into glue.
HEARD l16 The whole city got sticky. Subway seats, payphones, everything.
HEARD l17 The company's excuse, a bomb planted by anarchists.
HEARD l18 three years of hearings later, the court said no. It was just a bad tank.
HEARD l19 The harbor stayed brown until summer, and the streets smelled sweet for decades.
HEARD l20 And what was all that molasses for? Making alcohol.
HEARD l21 The very next day, America approved the law that banned alcohol.
